aking Hydrogen Work in Zero Carbon Jet Engines\nPINES\nTranspiration Cooling\nFlagships\nCORDITE\nExCITE\nMaking Hydrogen Work in Zero Carbon Jet Engines\nPINES\nTranspiration Cooling\nThe Institute\nHistory\nCDT in Future Flight and Propulsion\nTraining\nISO 9001\nCurrent vacancies\nContact\nImpact\nResearch Impact\nSpin Outs\nPublic engagement\nOutreach\nRHTG and IWSST Workshops\nPeople\nAcademics\nSenior Research Staff\nResearch and Engineering\nProfessional & Technical\nStudents\nVisiting Academics\nVisiting Researchers\nPublications\nNews\nEngineering Science\nBack\nOxford Thermofluids Institute\nThe Institute\nTraining\nOxford Thermofluids Institute | Training\nTraining\nDoctoral (DPhil) Degree\nIn addition to the CDT in Future Power and Propulsion, applicants wishing to complete a full-time or part-time DPhil in thermofluids should apply for the DPhil in Engineering Science, indicating their interest in thermofluids and aerospace engineering as part of their personal statement.\nApplicants are encouraged to contact potential supervisors in their area(s) of interest to identify research projects that are best suited to their skillset and aspirations.\nSeveral scholarships are available to cover post-graduate tuition fees and living expenses. Applicants are encouraged to check which of these funding schemes they are eligible for, and to apply to be considered for them as part of the post-graduate application process.\nMaster by Research (MSc) Degree\nThe MSc by Research in Engineering Science provides graduates with the opportunity to develop knowledge, understanding and expertise in their chosen field of engineering research, well beyond the level of a taught master\u2019s degree, but without the requirement to complete as much novel research as a doctoral student. Students develop broad skills in relevant areas of mathematical and computational modelling, in the design and build of apparatus and software, and in data analytics and visualisation.\nUndergraduate (MEng) Degree\nThere is no separate undergraduate course in thermofluids/aerospace engineering but the subject can be studied as part of the general Engineering Science course offered by the University of Oxford where specialisation is possible in the latter years. Interested students should follow the normal application procedure for reading Engineering Science at the University of Oxford.\nThe OTI\nHistory\nVacancies\nPublications\nNews\nResearch\nResearch Themes\nResearch Groups\nResearch Facilities\nResearch Impact\nStudy\nCDT Programme\nPostgraduate studies\nUndergraduate studies\nPeople\nAcademics\nSenior Research Staff\nResearch and Engineering Staff\nProfessional & Technical Staff\nContact OTI\nAccessibility\nPrivacy Policy\nCookie Statement\nEquality and Diversity\nLegal notice\nBack to top", "links": [["Skip to main content", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/the-institute/training#Main"], ["", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/"], ["", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/"], ["Intranet", "https://unioxfordnexus.sharepoint.com/sites/ENGS-HUB-OTIHub"], ["Engineering Science", "https://eng.ox.ac.uk/"], ["Intranet", "https://unioxfordnexus.sharepoint.com/sites/ENGS-HUB-OTIHub"], ["", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/"], ["", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/"], ["Research", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/research"], ["Research Groups", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/research/research-groups"], ["Active Flow Control Group", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/research/research-groups/active-flow-control-group"], ["Aerothermal Science Group", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/research/research-groups/aerothermal-science-group"], ["Oxford Turbine Research Facility Group", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/research/research-groups/oxford-turbine-research-facility-group"], ["Computational Fluid Dynamics Group", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/research/research-groups/computational-fluid-dynamics-group"], ["High Heat Flux Cooling Group", "https://oti.eng.ox.ac.uk/research/research-groups/high-heat-flux-cooling-group"], ["High Speed Turbomachin